{"name":"Corinthian Pillar","hex":"#e1d1b1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"corinthian","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Corinthian (“a sailboat owner who helms his or her own boat in competitive racing”)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/corinthian"},{"term":"pillar","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large post, often used as supporting architecture.","Something resembling such a structure."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To provide with pillars or added strength as if from pillars."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pillar"}],"entries":[{"title":"Corinthian order","blocks":[{"text":"The Corinthian order is the last developed and most ornate of the three principal classical orders of Ancient Greek architecture and later used in Roman architecture and Nabataean architecture. The other two are the Doric order, which was the earliest, followed by the Ionic order. In Ancient Greek architecture, the Corinthian order follows the Ionic in almost all respects, other than the capitals of the columns, though this changed in Roman architecture."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Temple_of_Bacchus_at_Baalbek.jpg/330px-Temple_of_Bacchus_at_Baalbek.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corinthian_order"},{"title":"Column","blocks":[{"text":"A column or pillar in architecture and structural engineering is a structural element that transmits, through compression, the weight of the structure above to other structural elements below. In other words, a column is a compression member. The term column applies especially to a large round support with a capital and a base or pedestal, which is made of stone, or appearing to be so. A small wooden or metal support is typically called a post. Supports with a rectangular or other non-round section are usually called piers. Throughout architectural history, especially in Classical and Rennaisance styles, the column has been central to building design."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/National_Capitol_Columns_-_Washington%2C_D.C..jpg/330px-National_Capitol_Columns_-_Washington%2C_D.C..jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Column"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Al-Khazneh_%28The_Treasury%29_2%2C_Petra%2C_Jordan.jpg/500px-Al-Khazneh_%28The_Treasury%29_2%2C_Petra%2C_Jordan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Al-Khazneh_%28The_Treasury%29_2%2C_Petra%2C_Jordan.jpg/1280px-Al-Khazneh_%28The_Treasury%29_2%2C_Petra%2C_Jordan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Al-Khazneh_%28The_Treasury%29_2%2C_Petra%2C_Jordan.jpg/500px-Al-Khazneh_%28The_Treasury%29_2%2C_Petra%2C_Jordan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Nabataean variation of Corinthian columns, from the Alkhazneh, Petra, unknown architect, early first century AD[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAl-Khazneh_(The_Treasury)_2%2C_Petra%2C_Jordan.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/The_Pantheon%2C_Rome_%2814811596068%29.jpg/500px-The_Pantheon%2C_Rome_%2814811596068%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/The_Pantheon%2C_Rome_%2814811596068%29.jpg/1280px-The_Pantheon%2C_Rome_%2814811596068%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/The_Pantheon%2C_Rome_%2814811596068%29.jpg/500px-The_Pantheon%2C_R